INT. WIZARD'S LIBRARY - THE ROOM OF TURNING SHELVES - NIGHT

Shelves rotate on slow brass gears. Books drift between them on their own. A fire burns in a hearth with no chimney. Candles hover.

ARCHIVIST THESSALY MOORE, 40s, ink-stained sleeves, spectacles on a cord, kneels at a lectern, copying a rune. Beside her, apprentice KESTREL DONOVAN-ABIOLA, 16, holds a lantern. Both ignore the voice murmuring through the walls.

VOICE (V.O.)
(from the stone itself)
Found you.

Thessaly's quill stops. Kestrel steps closer to her.

KESTREL
Was that the library?

THESSALY
The library doesn't speak.

VOICE (V.O.)
Oh, but I do. Thessaly Moore. You took something that is mine.

THESSALY
(not looking up)
I borrowed a book.

VOICE (V.O.)
A book. Yes. Open to the last page and read me what it says.

Kestrel pulls a leather volume from the shelf. It is warm in her hands. She flips to the last page.

KESTREL
(reading)
"Whoever opens this book wakes the one who wrote it."

THESSALY
Put it back. Slowly.

Kestrel reaches. The shelf rotates away from her. The book will not return to its slot.

VOICE (V.O.)
Do not trouble yourself. I am already awake.

The candles gutter. In the center of the room, the air thickens. A tall figure forms in a coat of gray feathers. A RAVEN-HEADED STRANGER, calm, hands clasped. Its beak glints.

RAVEN-HEADED STRANGER
Good evening. I am the Scribe.

THESSALY
I know who you are.

SCRIBE
Then you know why I came.

Kestrel edges behind the lectern. Thessaly rises, quill still in hand.

THESSALY
The pages were never yours. They belonged to the kingdom.

SCRIBE
I wrote the kingdom. Every war. Every winter. Every funeral. It is all mine.

KESTREL
(quietly)
Thessaly, his shadow is moving wrong.

It is. The shadow on the floor writes in the air, slow letters curling in black smoke.

THESSALY
Don't read it.

KESTREL
I can't help it.

She reads. Her eyes widen.

KESTREL (CONT'D)
It says my name.

SCRIBE
Kestrel Donovan-Abiola. Born in the ninth year of the drought. Died in the eleventh page of tonight.

THESSALY
(stepping between them)
Then turn the page.

SCRIBE
I would, if you could stop me.

Thessaly raises the quill. The tip ignites white. She draws a line across the air. A bright barrier of light slices the room.

The Scribe flinches. Feathers scatter.

SCRIBE (CONT'D)
(a hiss)
That ink is older than I am.

THESSALY
Good. It remembers you.

The shelves spin faster. The book in Kestrel's hands flares gold. She slams it shut. The Scribe shrieks.

SCRIBE
Give it back!

KESTREL
(holding it close)
No.

The hearth fire roars. The room tilts. Candles fall like stars.

SCRIBE (V.O.)
(already fading)
Then I will wait. Stories do.

The figure dissolves into black ink that runs down the wall and soaks into the stone. The shelves slow. The candles steady.

Silence. Thessaly lowers her quill, trembling. Kestrel lets out a breath.

KESTREL
Is it over?

THESSALY
Not while the book is open.

Kestrel looks down. The cover is warm. Something inside it beats.
